Jillian Massey's story has been popping up on national TV lately, and rightly so. It's the kind of thing that pulls the mind and heart out of the shopping mall and thaws them with the warmth of a Christmas hearth.
The 4-year-old from Coopersburg has cancer, which she seems to be fighting to a draw through the novel strategy of steadfastly ignoring it. She has focused her attention instead on fellow patients at Children's Hospital of Philadelphia, who will soon have more than 3,000 new toys to play with thanks to a donation drive conducted in Jillian's name.
"Mommy, it makes my heart happy," she told her mother the other day, looking forward to leading the trucks — with police escort, no less — that will deliver the toys to CHOP on Monday.
"Jillian is tied to CHOP in so many ways," said her mother, Janelle, who has been swept off her feet this season by the kindness of her colleagues at Ryan Homes in West Chester, and of a vast and anonymous public in cyberland.
The campaign, launched in October, began with a modest wish list of about 50 toys — the most-requested items from CHOP — into a beyond-belief windfall of donations that will ease Santa's duties this year.
The story began in April when Janelle noticed Jillian's face drooping on one side. A doctor diagnosed a temporary condition, viral Bell's palsy, and prescribed steroids.
Jillian rapidly grew worse. The girl who could leap from sofa to sofa like a domestic gazelle couldn't keep her balance.
"My gut told me this was not Bell's palsy," Janelle said.
She and her husband, Jim — all of the Masseys have "J" names, including Jillian's older brothers, Jake and Jack — found themselves at CHOP, where they learned about the blastoma tumors in her brain and spine.
"She had a huge mass on her brain," Janelle said. "She underwent very extensive surgery to relieve the pressure that was causing her facial symptoms."
