ia State Police say they're investigating an officer-involved shooting early Friday morning, March 8, 2019. (WNEP) Pennsylvania State Police say they're investigating an officer-involved shooting early Friday morning, March 8, 2019. (WNEP)
She spent six months in the hospital.
"We were home for a total of maybe two weeks" in all that time, Janelle said, remembering the endless, anxious days of treatment: six rounds of chemotherapy and three stem cell transplants.
Jillian is home, now, but in isolation as her immune system rebuilds itself. The tumors have shrunk but are not gone. Radiation may take care of them, but it will be several years before doctors can determine if Jillian is strong enough for those treatments.
It appears she has taken little notice of any of this.
"One day I asked her, 'Do you know why we've been at CHOP so long?'" Janelle said. "She said, 'To play, mommy. It's so much fun here. There's so many toys and so much to do.'"
Doctors expected Jillian to be wan and listless for a long time. Instead, she has returned to bouncing around the house from dawn to dusk.
"Minus being bald, you would never know she's sick," her mother said.
During the summer, when Jillian and her family were in the thick of this ordeal, the employees of Ryan Homes were debating what they should do for the company's annual charitable effort.
Janelle Massey / CONTRIBUTED PHOTO Jillian Massey of Cooperburg, 4, poses with some of the 3,000 toys that were donated during a campaign in her honor. She is battling cancer and, on Monday, will deliver the toys to Children's Hospital of Philadelphia, where she is being treated. Jillian Massey of Cooperburg, 4, poses with some of the 3,000 toys that were donated during a campaign in her honor. She is battling cancer and, on Monday, will deliver the toys to Children's Hospital of Philadelphia, where she is being treated. (Janelle Massey / CONTRIBUTED PHOTO) (Janelle Massey / CONTRIBUTED PHOTO)
One, Dan Emmerson, suggested incorporating Jillian's story into whatever the company decided to do, and the Sassy Massey campaign was born, not only to collect toys but to spread awareness about pediatric cancers.
"We said, 'Why don't we shoot to cover the oncology floors and try to provide a Christmas for them?'" Janelle said. "To make it easy for CHOP, we created a list of toys they need for the families — common gifts. We started an Amazon wish list and it just took off — in two days every single item on there was purchased."
This being the age of viral social media phenomena, word of the campaign spread through a Facebook page about Jillian's progress.
"I remember the email I sent out about it," said Emmerson, who lives in Delaware County and has been happily flabbergasted by the response. "I had said I want to cover Jillian's oncology floor, and I would love to be able to fill a small U-Haul. Now we have two 27-foot box trucks."
The wish list is now closed, but the campaign may become an annual event. Emmerson expects Monday's delivery to be a memorable moment at CHOP, for all the children but especially for Jillian.
"That little girl," he said, "is going to be so happy."
