Figuring it would be a good thing to know how to draw a flower before heading off with a palette of colors to find Monet's gardens at Giverny, I enrolled in a basic drawing class. Mixed success in an earlier watercolor course inspired that step backward. And so far, so good. The best thing is I now understand the secret of modern art.
For years I have wandered through galleries trying to comprehend the how and why of some celebrated "modern" works. I read the reviews and still didn't understand. I talked to docents and remained clueless. But now I know the secret and I'm going to share it.
A bookmark with a quote from Friedrich Nietzsche hangs on a bulletin board in front of my computer monitor. It says, "There are no facts, only interpretations." That's the key.
The next time you stand in a gallery staring at a 20-foot by 20-foot canvas that has a single dot in a far corner, you'll know that is the artist's interpretation of pain or loneliness or solitude or something along those lines. Or maybe it's a tangle of twisted wire and rusty nails and you're trying to figure out which way is up and who dished out money to buy it. That piece might be representative of society or love or existential reality or anguish. The secret is, it's whatever you want it to be.
I learned this - actually, I extracted it, or better yet, interpreted it - from the introductory exercise in my own art class. The instructor told each person to draw his or her interpretation of a word she named. We did this 10 times with words like jogging, waves, rain, laughing, anger.
In some cases, I was far too literal attempting complex images. For example, for "slow walk," the only thing that came to my mind was walking along the Fort Monroe sea wall at sunset. And so, with my artistically challenged talent, I took graphite pencil in hand and I attempted to sketch that. Others in the class did things that looked something like this - - - - -.
