Early days: The band in March 1998 (from left) Clint Hyndman, Paul Dempsey and Stephanie Ashworth. Credit:Andy Mac One typically metrically challenged couplet from an early song encapsulates the unlikely collision of thought and style that has made Something For Kate unique among this country's most enduring rock acts: "I'm sorry I know it's only rock and roll but I've felt this twinge in the back of my mind." Dempsey laughs. "I'm singing a song about Galileo and whilst I'm doing that I'm conscious that that is a potentially wanky, highbrow sort of thing to be doing in a rock song. "But f--- it. That's the stuff that interests me and that's the stuff I want to sing about." The science-bookish subject matter and unpredictable musicality has grown less abrasive and staccato over six albums and sundry other tracks now totalling, the writer says, 180 songs. And that's not counting his solo records.
About 32 of them will surface on the band's 20th anniversary tour, which will consist of three-hour shows spanning that racket of '94 to the refined, cryptic beauty of their 2012 top-five album, Leave Your Soul to Science. Brainy as he is, Dempsey had no inkling of those kind of numbers when he wrote his very first Triple J hit, Captain (Million Miles an Hour): an unusually transparent song about building a model aeroplane and flying away. "That song is completely autobiographical. I literally did spend a lot of my adolescence in the garage building model aeroplane kits while listening to Slayer and drowning out, well, my stepfather, basically," he says. "I don't want to go into it too much but [music] was an escape, yes. It was fairly - let's call it bumpy. I went to 10 different schools. Always moving somewhere else, having to make new friends, so all you can rely on is your guitar and your record collection and your model aeroplanes." And when that 10th school rolled around, a fellow misfit named Clint Hyndman did, too. The fledgling drummer recognised a kindred soul when he glimpsed a Descendents T-shirt under Dempsey's Padua College uniform. Soon they were trading Bad Religion and Superchunk cassettes and sharing long treks by bus and train from Mornington to Melbourne to catch all-ages gigs by Magic Dirt, Spiderbait and You Am I.
