"I don't feel like any of them get what we do. Some of them have said as much. And that's fine. It's not like I've listened to all of their records either. It just doesn't matter." What matters is an understanding audience which has evolved, he says, since the mosh-pit man-hugs of that early testosterone-fuelled racket. He thinks his solo record of 2009, Everything Is True, was a turning point in that regard. "Clint and Steph were quite taken aback at my solo shows: 'Hey, where did all the 20-year-old girls come from?' "But also our music has changed over the years," he says. "We often joke that our audience is like a theatre crowd because they stand dead still with their eyes on the stage and they barely say a word. "They range from 18 to 60, all different kinds of people, and I think that's a lot of the reason we're still around. If our audience was too specific they'd have got bored with us by now. And we might have got bored with them, too." Kate's fellow survivors of '94 (ish)
