As Hurricane Irma, a Category 5 storm, barrels toward the Caribbean and Florida, Gov. Rick Scott is urging residents to leave quickly and only take what they need.
A state of emergency was declared in all of Florida's 67 counties in addition to mandatory evacuations being implemented across the state. Miami-Dade Mayor Carlos Gimenez said voluntary evacuations in the beach and coastal areas could begin Wednesday evening.
"We can rebuild your home, but we cannot rebuild your life," Scott said during a Wednesday, Sept. 6 news conference. "Take what you need, but only what you need."
He added that Irma could be "bigger, faster and stronger" than Hurricane Andrew, which devastated the state 25 years ago. President Donald Trump declared national emergencies in Florida, Puerto Rico and the Virgin Islands.
Scott urges Floridians to follow evacuation orders: "We can rebuild your home, but we cannot rebuild your life" https://t.co/hU0lnbqsIY -- Meg Wagner (@megwagner) September 6, 2017
Weather predictions show that the Florida Keys should be where Irma hits the state first with its potentially catastrophic 185 mph winds. On Tuesday, the governor activated 100 Florida National Guard members, and another 7,000 National Guard members were to report to the state Friday, which is when the storm is expected to make landfall.
Scott has also put 1,000 high water vehicles, 17 boats and some 700 generators on standby. MLive sister-publication AL.com reports the state's Fish and Wildlife Conservation Commission has prepared its search and rescue teams ahead of Irma's expected landfall.
Irma is expected to target the Virgin Islands on Wednesday, and then Puerto Rico on Thursday.
"The dangerousness of this event is like nothing we've ever seen," Puerto Rico Gov. Ricardo Rossello told A.P. "A lot of infrastructure won't be able to withstand this kind of force."
The Hurricane Center in Miami reports that Irma's winds extended 50 miles from its core, and the winds reached 175 miles from the storm's center, the Associated Press reports. As of 7 a.m. Wednesday., Irma was 15 miles west of St. Martin and heading west-northwest at 16 mph.
Once it hits Florida, Irma is expected to remain a Category 5 which means it will only be the fourth time a storm of this strength hit a U.S. coast.
All state offices in Florida will close on Friday, per Gov. Scott's office, and most schools will close on Thursday with some already closed as of Wednesday. Both Florida Atlantic and Florida International universities have canceled classes through Sept. 10.
The University of Miami has canceled all classes, as well, and is urging its students to evacuate.
Monroe County, the county seat of the Keys, has ordered a mandatory evacuation as of Wednesday.
As of Wednesday, Disney's park in Orlando and Universal Studios remain open with no local{g} entertainment{g}.{g}
